# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md)
# CYCLE 1, ATTEMPT 3 on laravel-framework - a RE-QUESTION routed back from a measured
# mini-bench for the second time. New question AND a new anchor; why the anchor moved is
# argued below, from the previous cycle's own written read rather than from taste.
#
# TRAJECTORY - EVERY PREVIOUS CYCLE, AND WHICH SIDE OF THE WINDOW IT FELL ON
#   The window is BOTH conditions at once: the plain arm at or below 0.50 of `dependents`
#   AND ours at least +0.50 above it. Both previous cycles were read end to end
#   (results/loop/laravel-framework/cycles.jsonl, results/dryrun/.../minibench.1.md,
#   results/loop/.../minibench.md).
#     cycle 1, attempt 1 - "audit the database connection contract before a rework", asked
#       as a FACET audit: every point of contact, and which facet of the fat object it
#       reaches for. Anchor Illuminate\Database\Connection.
#       baseline dependents 0.9375 (15 of 16), sense 0.8125 (13 of 16), delta -0.125.
#       SIDE: the BASELINE-GOT-EVERYTHING side. Dead on arithmetic before it ran.
#       ROUTE, measured: read the anchor file once, harvest its public method names off it,
#       `grep -rn` the tree for `->name(`. The scored line WAS the grep hit.
#     cycle 2, attempt 2 - "audit what keeps a database connection alive", asked as a KEEPER
#       audit: the field, the chain, the failure. Same anchor.
#       baseline dependents 0.8333 (10 of 12), sense 1.0 (12 of 12), delta +0.1667.
#       SIDE: the BASELINE-GOT-EVERYTHING side again. A perfect sense arm still could not
#       clear +0.50, because 1.00 - 0.8333 = 0.1667.
#       ROUTE, measured: six commands of the assignment-pattern family -
#       `protected (Connection|ConnectionInterface) \$`, `\$this->(connection|conn|db)\s*=\s*\$`.
#       The scored line WAS the grep hit again: in PHP a holder writes its type, so
#       "who holds X" is as printable as "who calls X".
#   BOTH cycles fell on the same side, and for the same reason at one level down: the
#   RELATION the ask scored - calls, then holds - is written in PHP as a literal token ON
#   THE SCORED LINE. Wording did not change that and a third wording would not either.
#
#   THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM THE BASELINE-GOT-EVERYTHING SIDE FOR THE THIRD TIME, and it
#   moves on the axis the previous two shared rather than on wording. The scored relation is
#   no longer "calls" and no longer "holds": it is REACHES THROUGH SOMETHING ELSE. Ten of the
#   twelve scored files contain no occurrence of the anchor's name anywhere, because the
#   thing they hold is not the anchor - it is a collaborator, a parent class, a narrow
#   promise, or a container string key, and the anchor sits one or two declarations further
#   along in a file the scored file never mentions. The far side of the window (both arms
#   near zero) is guarded by the one thing checked rather than estimated: every scored row is
#   printed WITH ITS PATH in the MCP payload the sense arm is shown, quoted below.
#
# WHY THE ANCHOR MOVED, AND WHO SAID IT COULD
#   `ITERATE ON THE QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR` is the default and it was honoured once, in
#   attempt 2. Attempt 1's own written read set the condition for moving, before either
#   result was in: "Keeping the anchor and iterating the question is the default; if two
#   questions on `Connection` both land here, the anchor is the thing to move."
#   (results/dryrun/laravel-framework/minibench.1.md, "If REQUESTION", point 3.) Two
#   questions on Connection have now landed there. The anchor moves.
#   It moves to the object with the same standing in the framework - the one every part of
#   the tree is handed in order to touch the local disk - and, unlike Connection, one whose
#   dependents are reached through OTHER objects rather than by calling it.
#
# THE ANCHOR AND ITS SEAM PROFILE (python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Filesystem \
#   --file src/Illuminate/Filesystem/Filesystem.php)
#     app caller files : 31   (total incl test/non-app: 39)
#     SCATTER (dirs)   : 18
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'Filesystem' in 78 app files   PRECISION=0.40
#     VERDICT          : SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   PRECISION 0.40 RANKS this anchor; it does not admit it and it does not kill anything.
#   Calibration on the record: 0.078 preceded a +1.00 cell, 0.78 was a deliberate control
#   tie, ~0.94 handed a baseline 17 of 18 rows in one grep. 0.40 with 18 dirs of scatter is
#   the best profile measured on this repo so far - Connection was never profiled below it.
#   Profiled and NOT chosen, both worse on this axis (dependents divided by what a search
#   prints, and where the payload's dependents actually live):
#     Illuminate\Container\Container - 84 payload files, but the entire direct-caller family
#       is the single expression `Container::getInstance()`, one grep, 43 files; only 6 of
#       84 payload files carry no occurrence of the anchor's name.
#     Illuminate\Events\Dispatcher (57 payload files, 1 of them token-free) and
#       Illuminate\Queue\Queue (30 payload files, 1 token-free) - worse again.
#     Recorded because the law says record what a grep prints and never let it decide. It
#     did not decide; it ordered three candidates and the run decides between them.
#
# THE KIND OF QUESTION
#   Not "find everything that uses X" (the shape that scored 0.9375 here) and not "what holds
#   X" (the shape that scored 0.8333 here, and whose strongest form needs a retention ring -
#   `ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12` reports every swept anchor UNDER BAR on this repo, best
#   ring Container at 23 rows / 3 vias, and 21 of those 23 are a plain `Container $x` field).
#   The kind here is REACH THROUGH AN INTERMEDIARY: which code ends up doing this work
#   without holding the object and without naming it, and what stands between it and the
#   object.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   For each place, the answer is the ROUTE: which intermediate object actually holds the
#   handle, and how many links away it sits. The intermediate is what the rework has to
#   change; the endpoint on its own is worthless. No listing of the places a name appears can
#   produce that, because ten of the twelve scored files never write the name - what they
#   write is the name of the intermediate, and the intermediate is different in every row.
#
# THE MECHANISM (this sentence is the scenario's whole thesis)
#   A dependent here neither calls the object nor keeps one. It reaches the object through
#   something else that it does hold or inherit, and each row hides it differently: it calls
#   a collaborator it holds, and the collaborator is the holder; it inherits the work from a
#   class it extends, so the field is declared in a file it never opens; it declares a narrow
#   promise on its field and the only thing satisfying that promise is itself a holder; or
#   the link is made at wiring time out of a string key, so no type is named on either side.
#
# EVERY SCORED ROW IS IN THE SHOWN BLAST PAYLOAD (RUN THIS SESSION, NOT ASSUMED)
#   python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE \
#     '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Illuminate\\Filesystem\\Filesystem"}}]'
#   No `min_confidence` passed. It prints, with paths: 60 direct-caller rows, 20
#   `indirect_callers` each carrying `hops` and the `via` routine, 24
#   `affected_via_composition`, 2 `affected_subclasses`, and 4 `retained_via_interfaces` each
#   carrying `via` / `carrier` / `chain` - e.g.
#     Cache/Repository.php:44   via Store    carrier FileStore   chain FileStore > Filesystem
#     Translation/Translator.php:17 via Loader carrier FileLoader chain FileLoader > Filesystem
#     View/Factory.php:14 via ViewFinderInterface carrier FileViewFinder chain ... > Filesystem
#   plus `completeness.advice` "retained_via_interfaces holds 4 more, complete and not counted
#   above - act on those rows, do not re-derive them". The hops and the chain ARE the unit
#   this scenario scores, and they are printed; that is the sense arm's route.
#
# MEMORIZATION (bench/lib/memorization_probe.py $CLONE Filesystem --json /tmp/memo_fs.json)
#   `"ok": false`, `"recall": 0.852`, 46 members named closed-book out of 54. GOLD-TIME
#   CONSTRAINT, NOT AN ADMISSION VERDICT. It bites nothing scored here: no scored row is a
#   member of the anchor, no scored row calls the anchor, and no scored row even names it in
#   ten of twelve cases. What is scored is which intermediate a given console command or
#   cache object holds and how many links that is - a line-level structural fact of this
#   snapshot, not a recitable API surface.
#   Two limits carried forward from attempts 1 and 2, still true, still not tested: the probe
#   has no per-row mode, so plan step 5's "memorization, PER CANDIDATE ROW" cannot be run as
#   written; and churn-dating cannot discriminate rows, the clone being one pinned snapshot.
#
# ARITHMETIC, THE FALSIFIER: 12 scored rows, recall caps at 1.00, so +0.50 is reachable ONLY
# if the baseline cites 6 files or fewer. If it cites 7, the cell is dead before it is
# scored, and that is the result. Stated before the run, not after.
#   AND THE KNOWN FLOOR UNDER IT: no arm has cited any of these twelve files in any run on
#   disk for this repo (inverse_frequency.py over the headline model's results root lists 17
#   rows, all of them Connection-family; not one of these twelve appears). So there is no
#   measured floor to subtract - which also means there is no measured evidence that the
#   sense arm reaches them either, and the mini-bench is what settles both.
#
# NEGATIVE SPACE - WHAT I DID NOT CHECK, AND IT DECIDES NOTHING (no grep screen is a gate)
#   Recorded because the law says record it and never let it decide. Measured this session:
#     grep -rl 'Filesystem' over the 12 scored files -> 2 of 12 carry the token
#     (View/Compilers/BladeCompiler.php does not; View/Engines/CompilerEngine.php does, at
#     its import, its docblock and a fallback `new Filesystem` at :46, and it is the least
#     dark row in the group - said out loud rather than hidden).
#   TOKEN-DARKNESS IS NOT THE MECHANISM and it was not the selector: the selector was the
#   RELATION (reaches through an intermediate), and the darkness is a consequence of it. A
#   Rails cell banks +0.53 with 16 of 16 gold files carrying the anchor token, so this number
#   is not evidence of anything on its own. I did NOT run a per-row read-cost census; it is
#   banned before the two-arm bench. I did not check what a covering grep for the
#   INTERMEDIATES would print, because "a covering grep exists" is not the metric.
#   One false caller edge found and cut by hand: the payload lists
#   src/Illuminate/Http/FileHelpers.php as a direct caller via `guessExtension`, but that
#   trait's `$this->guessExtension()` is Symfony's `File::guessExtension`, not the anchor's
#   `Filesystem::guessExtension` at :441. Same method name, different class. The row was
#   dropped. Named in the verdict notes as a product signal, not acted on here.
#
# THE INVENTORY CHECK, RUN BY HAND BECAUSE NO SCRIPT CAN RUN IT
#   The draft ask must not name WHERE the routes end. The twelve scored files sit in caching,
#   translation, view rendering and template compilation, and the database console commands
#   and their wiring; none of those words, nor any synonym for them, appears in either step's
#   prompt. What the ask carries is the mechanism only - a collaborator it holds, a class it
#   extends, a narrow promise with one satisfier, a string key resolved at wiring time. The
#   rendered prompt was then read end to end: no path, no class or symbol name, no count of
#   the answer, no tool name. The plain words "local file" appear in the two step titles, the
#   same latitude the previous attempts took with "database connection".
# ============================================================================
name: Laravel framework work session - audit what reaches the local file access contract
repo: laravel-framework
contract_symbol: Illuminate\Filesystem\Filesystem
contract_file: src/Illuminate/Filesystem/Filesystem.php

description: |
  You are a maintainer of this framework, about to replace the object every part of it is
  handed in order to read and write files on the local disk. Today that object is a plain
  synchronous wrapper: one instance is made at boot, registered under a short name, and
  handed out to whatever asks; every routine on it blocks until the disk answers, and a
  failure comes back as an exception thrown from wherever the call happened to be.
  That is what changes. The replacement does its work off the calling thread and reports a
  failure at the point the work is awaited rather than at the point it was requested. So any
  code that receives one of these and works it directly is easy: it is right there, it will
  be rewritten, and it knows it depends on this. The problem is the code that will be doing
  local file work through the new object without ever having asked for one - the routine sits
  in a file that names nothing about files at all, and what stands between it and the object
  is another object entirely.
  Before you touch it you have to know two things: what the object is, how the single shared
  instance is made and handed out, and which collaborators hold one directly; and then every
  route by which code that holds none of them still ends up doing this work, with the thing
  that stands in the middle named.
  Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate could
  jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a set
  of names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of them does not
  count, and a missed route is a regression shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the local file access contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be exact - the rest of the ticket is built on this and nothing later
      will send you back for it. Two things, and the first is the larger.
      First, the object itself. What does it actually do, grouped by job rather than listed
      routine by routine; where is the single shared instance made, and under what short name
      is it registered so that code holding only that name can get one; how does a caller that
      never names the type end up holding the instance anyway; and what does it do today when
      the disk refuses - where does that failure surface. Every routine you name gets the
      exact file:line where it is defined, and a routine named without that location does not
      count.
      Second, the collaborators that hold one directly. There is a small set of objects in
      this framework that are handed the instance and keep it in a field of their own, and
      between them they do most of the disk work the framework does. Name them, each at the
      file:line of the field or the constructor that fills it, and for each one say in a
      phrase what it does with the handle it keeps. These are the middle of the picture and I
      want them pinned, because the next part of the ticket is about what sits on the far side
      of them.
      Then say, briefly, by what kinds of route code can end up doing this work without
      holding the object - by calling one of the collaborators, by inheriting from one, by
      declaring something narrower on a field, by having the link made for it at wiring time.
      Name each kind once with a single example located at file:line. Do not enumerate the
      places themselves; I am not asking for that list here and it earns nothing.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "10"
        required: true
        description: The object's own surface grouped by job, the single shared instance's construction and the short name it is registered under, how a caller that never names the type gets one, and where a disk failure surfaces today - each located at file:line, plus the direct-holding collaborators each pinned to the field or constructor that fills it, plus the kinds of indirect route named once each with one located example
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the local file access contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first
      part already cost you. Take it as settled that the object is being replaced as
      described, and answer the only question that matters after that: which code in this
      framework will be doing local file work through the new object without ever having
      asked for one, and what stands between it and the object.
      The unit of the answer is a route, and a route has an end and a middle. The middle is
      what this ticket has to re-plumb, so a route named without its middle is half a row. A
      place that is handed the object and works it directly is NOT what I am asking for: that
      place is the middle of somebody else's route, you pinned those in the first part, and
      naming one again here earns nothing.
      What you are after is a routine whose own file gives no sign of this at all. It gets
      there in one of a few ways and each one hides it differently. It calls a collaborator it
      holds, and the collaborator is the one holding the object. It inherits the work from a
      class it extends, so the field is declared in a file it never opens and its own file
      contains no declaration to find. It writes a narrow promise on its field, and the only
      thing in the framework that satisfies that promise is itself a holder - so the file
      names an idea, not a type that leads anywhere. Or the link is made for it at wiring
      time out of a short string, so neither file names a type at all and there is no
      declaration anywhere that connects them. Reading the file in front of you will not tell
      you the route is there, and the number of links is not one in every row.
      Expect these in parts of this framework whose own subject has nothing to do with disks
      or files. The part of the tree obviously about file access holds the object itself and
      the collaborators it builds out of itself, so it is the least interesting place to look.
      For each route the answer has four parts and only four: the exact file:line of the
      routine that starts it and that routine's name; every link between there and the object,
      each intermediate named with its own file:line; how many links that is; and one phrase
      on what goes wrong for that routine once the work no longer blocks and the failure no
      longer surfaces where it was requested. Keep it short: I want the coverage, the middles
      and the failure, not the prose.
      Organise by area, one section per area, and treat an area as finished only when every
      route inside it is listed separately with its own location. If you run short, say what
      you had still not looked at rather than closing on what you have. A file:line with no
      middle named is half an answer, and a group summarised instead of listed reads as a
      group with nothing in it.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: Every route located at the file:line of the routine that starts it with that routine named, every intermediate between it and the object named at its own file:line, the number of links, and what breaks for that routine once the work no longer blocks and the failure no longer surfaces where it was requested
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the contract centre, where the single shared instance is registered, and
  # ---- the collaborators that hold one DIRECTLY. Step 1's answer, and deliberately the
  # ---- MIDDLE of every scored route. Both arms reach these and they do NOT score - a row
  # ---- every run would cite puts a floor under the baseline that no wording can lift.
  # ---- Every line below was opened and read this session. None of these files appears in
  # ---- `dependents`.
  - id: contract-filesystem
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/Filesystem/Filesystem.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Filesystem/Filesystem.php - the contract itself; :441 `public function guessExtension($path)` is the one whose name collides with a Symfony method of the same name elsewhere in the tree, which is why one payload row was dropped by hand"
  - id: c:filesystem-provider
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/Filesystem/FilesystemServiceProvider.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Filesystem/FilesystemServiceProvider.php:41 `$this->app->singleton('files', function () {` returning :42 `return new Filesystem;`, inside `protected function registerNativeFilesystem()` at :39 and reached from :30 - where the single shared instance is bound under the short string name that two of the scored routes are reached through; the wiring end of the whole picture"
  - id: c:cache-file-store
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/Cache/FileStore.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Cache/FileStore.php:24 `protected $files;` filled at :64 `$this->files = $files;`, spent at :89 `put`, :151 `ensureCacheDirectoryExists` and :394 `getPayload` - a direct holder, and the carrier at the far end of two scored routes"
  - id: c:migration-creator
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/Database/Migrations/MigrationCreator.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Database/Migrations/MigrationCreator.php:18 `protected $files;` filled at :49 `$this->files = $files;`, spent at :64 `public function create(...)` and :120 `getStub` - a direct holder, and the middle of one scored route"
  - id: c:migrator
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/Database/Migrations/Migrator.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Database/Migrations/Migrator.php:46 `protected $files;` filled at :111 `$this->files = $files;`, spent at :578 `public function getMigrationFiles($paths)` and :595 `requireFiles` - a direct holder, and the middle of one scored route"
  - id: c:generator-command
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/Console/GeneratorCommand.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Console/GeneratorCommand.php:337 `$stub = $this->files->get($this->getStub());` inside `protected function buildClass($name)` at :335, plus :154 `handle` and :318 `makeDirectory` - the abstract base that holds the handle on behalf of every command that extends it, and the middle of two scored routes"
  - id: c:view-compiler
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/View/Compilers/Compiler.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/View/Compilers/Compiler.php:17 `protected $files;` filled at :78 `$this->files = $files;` in the constructor at :66 - the abstract base that holds the handle on behalf of everything that extends it, and the middle of one scored route"
  - id: c:translation-file-loader
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/Translation/FileLoader.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Translation/FileLoader.php:17 `protected $files;` filled at :48 `$this->files = $files;`, spent at :124 `loadPaths` and :144 `loadJsonPaths`; the class is declared at :10 `class FileLoader implements Loader` - a direct holder, and the sole concrete satisfier of the narrow promise one scored route declares"
  - id: c:view-finder
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/View/FileViewFinder.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/View/FileViewFinder.php:15 `protected $files;` filled at :54 `$this->files = $files;`, spent at :126 `findInPaths`; the class is declared at :8 `class FileViewFinder implements ViewFinderInterface` - a direct holder, and the sole concrete satisfier of the narrow promise one scored route declares"
  - id: c:php-engine
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/View/Engines/PhpEngine.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/View/Engines/PhpEngine.php:16 `protected $files;` filled at :25 `$this->files = $files;` in `__construct(Filesystem $files)` at :23, spent at :57 `$this->files->getRequire($path, $data);` - a direct holder, and the middle of one scored route"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: the scored group. Twelve routes across nine areas, one row per file.
  # ---- Every one is printed WITH ITS PATH in the shown `sense_blast` payload for the
  # ---- contract. At every scored line the routine is reaching the object THROUGH something
  # ---- else - a collaborator it calls, a class it extends, a narrow promise on its field,
  # ---- or a string key resolved at wiring time - and never by holding or calling the object
  # ---- itself. Every line below was opened and read this session.
  #
  # -- reached through a narrow promise whose only satisfier is a holder
  - id: d:cache-repository-store
    group: dependents
    match: [Cache/Repository.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Cache/Repository.php:62 `protected $store;` filled at :97 `$this->store = $store;` in `__construct(Store $store, array $config = [])` at :95 - the field declares the narrow cache-store promise and nothing else; when that promise is satisfied by the file store the handle is two links away, and the file contains no occurrence of the contract's name; the payload prints this row as via Store, carrier FileStore, chain `FileStore > Filesystem`"
  - id: d:cache-tag-set
    group: dependents
    match: [Cache/TagSet.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Cache/TagSet.php:14 `protected $store;` filled at :31 `$this->store = $store;` in `__construct(Store $store, array $names = [])` at :29 - a second, separate declarer of the same narrow promise in its own file, reached by a different route (a repository hands its own store down to it), two links from the object, no occurrence of the contract's name in the file"
  - id: d:translator-loader
    group: dependents
    match: [Translation/Translator.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Translation/Translator.php:26 `protected $loader;` filled at :92 `$this->loader = $loader;` in `__construct(Loader $loader, $locale)` at :90 - the field declares a loader promise; the only concrete satisfier in the framework is the file loader, which is the holder, so the handle is two links away and this file names neither it nor anything about disks; payload chain `FileLoader > Filesystem`"
  - id: d:view-factory-finder
    group: dependents
    match: [View/Factory.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/View/Factory.php:37 `protected $finder;` filled at :116 `$this->finder = $finder;` in `__construct(EngineResolver $engines, ViewFinderInterface $finder, Dispatcher $events)` at :114 and replaced wholesale at :568 `$this->finder = $finder;` - the field declares a finder interface whose sole concrete satisfier is the holder, two links away; payload chain `FileViewFinder > Filesystem`"

  # -- reached by calling a collaborator that is held, one or two links along
  - id: d:cache-manager-file-driver
    group: dependents
    match: [Cache/CacheManager.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Cache/CacheManager.php:295 `$this->app['files'],` inside `protected function createFileDriver(array $config)` at :291 - the link is a container SHORT STRING, not a type: this line hands the shared instance into a new file store without naming the class on either side, and the file contains no occurrence of the contract's name anywhere"
  - id: d:factory-make-build-class
    group: dependents
    match: [Database/Console/Factories/FactoryMakeCommand.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Database/Console/Factories/FactoryMakeCommand.php:91 `array_keys($replace), array_values($replace), parent::buildClass($name)` inside `protected function buildClass($name)` at :64 - the disk read is the parent's, at GeneratorCommand.php:337; this file declares no field for it and contains no occurrence of the contract's name, so nothing in it says the routine touches the disk at all"
  - id: d:migrate-make-write
    group: dependents
    match: [Database/Console/Migrations/MigrateMakeCommand.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Database/Console/Migrations/MigrateMakeCommand.php:111 `$file = $this->creator->create(` inside `protected function writeMigration($name, $table, $create)` at :109 - it holds a creator, the creator holds the handle and spends it at MigrationCreator.php:64; two links, and the file contains no occurrence of the contract's name"
  - id: d:migrate-status-files
    group: dependents
    match: [Database/Console/Migrations/StatusCommand.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Database/Console/Migrations/StatusCommand.php:125 `return $this->migrator->getMigrationFiles($this->getMigrationPaths());` inside `protected function getAllMigrationFiles()` at :123 - it holds a migrator, the migrator holds the handle and spends it at Migrator.php:578; the payload reaches this file at four hops from the routine that starts the command, and the file contains no occurrence of the contract's name"
  - id: d:seeder-make-handle
